# Coding Standards for Python

## Key Principles

- you SHOULD prefer `pyproject.toml` for project metadata and dependencies
- if `uv` is installed, you SHOULD use it instead of `pip`
- you MUST follow [PEP 8](https://peps.python.org/pep-0008/) naming conventions
- you MUST add type hints on public APIs; you SHOULD prefer `list[str]` / `dict[str, T]` style (3.9+) over `typing.List` unless the project targets older Python
- you SHOULD prefer the standard library before adding a dependency

## Tooling

- you MUST use `ruff` when the project configures it or it is installed; you MUST NOT add it solely for a one-off edit
- if the project uses `pytest`, you MUST put tests next to existing test layout; you MUST NOT add a new test framework

|Check|Tool|Command|Notes|
|:-|:-|:-|:-|
|**Linting**|`ruff`|`ruff check`||
|**Formatting**|`ruff`|`ruff format --check`|`ruff check --select I` checks import order|
|**Type Checking**|`ty`|`ty check`|you SHOULD prefer the checker the project configures|

## Paradigms

### Error Handling

- you MUST raise specific exception types; you MUST NOT use bare `except:`
- you MUST use `raise ... from err` when re-raising to preserve context
- you MUST validate inputs at trust boundaries (CLI, network, file I/O)
